   479→### Anti-Patterns to Avoid
   480→
   481→- **Inconsistent color schemes across pages:** Portfolio (green) vs. project pages (purple) creates fragmented experience. Unify on one palette or create subtle variations of the same base colors.
   482→- **Pure vw units for typography:** Breaks browser zoom accessibility. Always combine with rem.
   483→- **Overly complex animations:** User requested "minimal and subtle" - avoid flashy transitions, parallax scrolling, or entrance animations.
   484→- **Deep folder nesting:** Keep structure flat within `/projects/graphics-engine/` - docs should be `/docs/page.html` not `/docs/section/category/page.html`.
   485→- **Hamburger menu without ARIA:** Screen readers need aria-expanded, aria-controls, and aria-label for accessibility.
   486→- **Fixed pixel breakpoints for everything:** Use content-driven breakpoints where design naturally needs adjustment, not arbitrary device widths.
   487→
   488→## Don't Hand-Roll
   489→
   490→| Problem | Don't Build | Use Instead | Why |
   491→|---------|-------------|-------------|-----|
   492→| Fluid typography calculations | Manual media queries for each text size | CSS clamp() with rem + vw | Native, accessible, smoother scaling, less code |
   493→| Design token management | Inline styles or Sass variables | CSS Custom Properties | Runtime-dynamic, inspector-visible, no build step |
   494→| Card alignment across varying content | Manual height adjustments or JavaScript | CSS Grid with subgrid | Native alignment, no layout shift, responsive |
   495→| Color palette generation | Manual color picking | Existing palette in CLAUDE.md | User has established brand colors |
   496→| Mobile menu drawer animation | Custom JavaScript animation | CSS transitions with transform | Smoother, GPU-accelerated, simpler code |
   497→| Accessible navigation patterns | Custom ARIA implementation | WCAG 2.1 patterns (researched) | Tested, documented, screen reader compatible |
   498→| Responsive breakpoints | Fixed device widths (320px, 768px, 1024px, 1440px) | Content-driven breakpoints | Adapts to actual content needs, not device assumptions |
   499→
   500→**Key insight:** Modern CSS has matured significantly. Features like clamp(), custom properties, Grid, and subgrid eliminate most needs for preprocessors or JavaScript-based styling solutions. For a static portfolio site, leveraging native CSS capabilities provides the best performance, maintainability, and accessibility outcomes.
   501→
   502→## Common Pitfalls
   503→
   504→### Pitfall 1: Color Palette Fragmentation
   505→
   506→**What goes wrong:** Current implementation has inconsistent gradients:
   507→- Landing page (index.html): Green gradient `#2E7D32` to `#1B5E20`
   508→- Demo/docs pages: Purple/blue gradient `#667eea` to `#764ba2`
   509→
   510→User explicitly requested "the whole website should flow with the same style."
   511→
   512→**Why it happens:** Pages were created separately without unified design system. CLAUDE.md mentions purple palette but actual portfolio uses green.
   513→
   514→**How to avoid:**
   515→1. Audit all existing color usage across all HTML files
   516→2. Decide on single primary gradient (recommend green from landing page as it's the "official" portfolio color)
   517→3. Replace all gradient/color declarations with CSS custom properties
   518→4. Use accent colors from secondary palette for variety (purple as accent, not primary)
   519→
   520→**Warning signs:**
   521→- Different `linear-gradient()` values across pages
   522→- Hard-coded hex values instead of variables
   523→- Visual disconnect when navigating from portfolio to project pages
   524→
   525→### Pitfall 2: Accessibility-Breaking Typography
   526→
   527→**What goes wrong:** Using pure `vw` units for font sizing breaks browser zoom functionality, failing WCAG 2.1 AA criteria for text resizing (must support 200% zoom).
   528→
   529→**Why it happens:** Developers see `font-size: 2vw` scales smoothly with viewport and assume it's the "modern" way. This ignores that `vw` is viewport-relative, not user-preference-relative.
   530→
   531→**How to avoid:**
   532→```css
   533→/* BAD - breaks zoom */
   534→h1 {
   535→  font-size: 2vw;
   536→}
   537→
   538→/* GOOD - respects zoom via rem */
   539→h1 {
   540→  font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 2vw, 3rem);
   541→}
   542→```
   543→
   544→Always include `rem` in the preferred value calculation. The `rem` component ensures zoom functionality works while `vw` provides viewport responsiveness.
   545→
   546→**Warning signs:**
   547→- Text doesn't increase when zooming browser to 200%
   548→- Font size inspector shows only `vw` units with no `rem`
   549→- Axe DevTools or WAVE accessibility scanner flags text resizing issues
   550→
   551→### Pitfall 3: Hamburger Menu Accessibility Gaps
   552→
   553→**What goes wrong:** Hamburger menus without proper ARIA attributes are unusable for screen reader users and keyboard-only navigation.
   554→
   555→**Why it happens:** Developers focus on visual design (three-line icon, drawer animation) but forget semantic HTML and ARIA requirements.
   556→
   557→**How to avoid:** Essential attributes checklist:
   558→- Button element (not `<div>` or `<a>`) for semantic meaning
   559→- `aria-expanded` attribute that toggles between "true"/"false"
   560→- `aria-controls` pointing to menu ID
   561→- `aria-label` describing button purpose ("Toggle navigation menu")
   562→- `role="menubar"` and `role="menuitem"` on menu and links
   563→- Keyboard support: Enter to toggle, Escape to close, Tab through links
   564→- Focus trap: when menu opens, focus moves into drawer; when closes, returns to toggle button
   565→
   566→**Warning signs:**
   567→- Can't operate menu with keyboard only
   568→- Screen reader announces "button" without context
   569→- No announcement when menu expands/collapses
   570→- Focus jumps unexpectedly or gets trapped
   571→
   572→### Pitfall 4: Breakpoint Overload
   573→
   574→**What goes wrong:** Defining media queries for every common device width (320px, 375px, 425px, 768px, 1024px, 1440px, 2560px) creates maintenance burden and doesn't address actual content needs.
   575→
   576→**Why it happens:** Developers copy "standard breakpoints" from frameworks or articles without considering their specific content.
   577→
   578→**How to avoid:**
   579→1. Start with no breakpoints
   580→2. Resize browser from mobile width upward
   581→3. Add breakpoint ONLY where design actually breaks or becomes awkward
   582→4. Name breakpoints by content need, not device: `@media (max-width: 768px)` → `/* When navigation needs to collapse */`
   583→
   584→For this portfolio, likely need only 2-3 breakpoints:
   585→- ~768px: Navigation collapses to hamburger, card grid goes single column
   586→- ~480px: Further reduce padding, smaller typography, demo controls stack vertically
   587→
   588→**Warning signs:**
   589→- More than 4-5 breakpoints in CSS
   590→- Breakpoints at exact device widths (375px = iPhone SE, 414px = iPhone Pro Max)
   591→- Multiple breakpoints with minimal style differences
   592→- Breakpoints that don't correspond to actual layout changes
   593→
   594→### Pitfall 5: Inline CSS Duplication
   595→
   596→**What goes wrong:** Current implementation has all CSS inline in each HTML file's `<style>` tag. Changing colors requires editing 10+ files. No shared design system.
   597→
   598→**Why it happens:** Quick iteration during development. Each page built independently. GitHub Pages static site means no build process assumed.
   599→
   600→**How to avoid:**
   601→1. Extract common styles to `assets/css/design-system.css`
   602→2. Link from all pages: `<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/design-system.css">`
   603→3. Keep page-specific styles in page (landing page hero, docs page tabs)
   604→4. Use CSS custom properties so changes to tokens propagate everywhere
   605→
   606→**Warning signs:**
   607→- Same CSS rules copied across multiple files
   608→- Inconsistent spacing values (padding: 1.5rem vs padding: 24px)
   609→- Can't make global style change without search-and-replace
   610→- Color values hard-coded instead of using variables
   611→
   612→### Pitfall 6: Navigation Context Confusion
   613→
   614→**What goes wrong:** Users can't tell if they're on portfolio site vs. inside graphics engine project. Unclear how to navigate between projects or back to portfolio.
   615→
   616→**Why it happens:** Flat site structure with no breadcrumbs or contextual navigation. All pages look like siblings.
   617→
   618→**How to avoid:**
   619→- Portfolio pages: Navigation has "Projects", "About", "Contact", "GitHub" (portfolio context)
   620→- Project pages: Navigation has breadcrumb ("Portfolio / Projects / Graphics Engine"), project links ("Demo", "Docs", "Source"), and "Back to Portfolio" link
   621→- Visual distinction: Breadcrumb shows hierarchy, different navigation layout indicates context shift
   622→- Consistent footer: All pages link back to portfolio root
   623→
   624→**Warning signs:**
   625→- Users ask "how do I get back to main site?"
   626→- Analytics show high bounce rate from project pages (can't find other content)
   627→- All pages have identical navigation regardless of context
   628→- No visual indicator of current location
